.TH BUFFER_PUTM_INTERNAL_FLUSH 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
buffer_putm_internal_flush \- Put multiple strings into a buffer and flush

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/buffer.h>

.IB int
.IB buffer_putm_internal_flush (buffer* b, ...);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBbuffer_putm_internal_flush\fR function puts multiple strings into a buffer and then flushes the buffer. It takes a variable number of arguments, each of which should be a string to be put into the buffer. The function returns 0 on success, or -1 if an error occurred.

The function takes the following parameters:
.IB buffer*
A pointer to the buffer structure.

The function returns 0 on success, or -1 if an error occurred.

The \fBbuffer_putm_internal_flush\fR function is intended to be used to put multiple strings into a buffer and then flush the buffer, such as when formatting multiple lines of output.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBbuffer_putm_internal_flush\fR function to put multiple strings into a buffer and then flush the buffer:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/buffer.h>

int main() {
    buffer* b = buffer_new();
    const char* line1 = "Hello, world!";
    const char* line2 = "This is a test.";

    if(buffer_putm_internal_flush(b, line1, line2, NULL) == -1) {
        perror("buffer_putm_internal_flush");
        buffer_free(b);
        return 1;
    }

    buffer_free(b);
    return 0;
}
.nef